From 1591ad57eccd3857b9a689aa662e09ebda7cd01f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 10:56:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(entities): evaluate custom actors with shared animation
 graphs

Use the resource-defined actor graph for custom entity bone poses rather
than a separate evaluator restricted to repeating channels and static
single-state controllers. Preserve initialize and pre-animation variables,
weighted references, controller transitions, finite and held animation
clocks, and animation sound and particle events. Retain explicit legacy
animation_controllers roots before modern script roots.

Keep playback per actor object and authoritative lifetime. Release it on
resource or world changes and actor removal. Sample once into immutable
render state before deferred draws, apply authored bone names without
humanoid aliases, and retain native full matrices for entity-relative
rotations. Resolve effect locators from the selected model, including actor
scale, with attachment callbacks guarded against stale lifetimes.

Evaluate controller UV expressions in the sampled actor's variable scope.
Preserve finite component defaults and the bounded immutable render-type
cache. Route player and attachable graph construction through the shared
server resource reader rather than duplicate animation resource loading.

The reference remains Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372, protocol 2193.
Target callback 14065a850 computes camera-minus-actor yaw as atan2(z,x) in
degrees minus 90 and pitch as negative atan2(y,horizontal). Keep the native
unwrapped yaw range. Native state blend 141e6fe70 confirms base scale times
animation scale. Saved CubeCraft and Hive packs exercise camera-facing
bones, held position and scale, and explicit legacy controller roots.
Their assets and executable inspection remain private.

This generalizes existing graph playback to custom actors. Server animation
commands use lifetime-bound core state and negotiated delivery. Resolve
command-only aliases and global resources lazily, retain named states and
transitions, queue the final valid selection before a frame, and reset
repeated selections with the outgoing state blend curve. Create runtime
slots in the order of their first resolvable commands so ignored commands
cannot change shared-variable evaluation order.

Keep native query binding to the state selected before the update,
including forced selections. Nested controllers clear that binding.
Apply commands to custom actor and player graphs, including animation
effect callbacks. First-person graph sampling uses independent clocks but
its current hand renderer does not yet apply sampled bone poses. Ordinary
mob drawing and visible native timing comparisons remain required work.
Passing semantic tests alone does not establish complete visible actor or
animation parity.

Native evidence includes the target state builder 141bbe700, transition
insertion 141e6bfa0, selection 141e6bb40, and controller update 141e6d180.
Executable probes establish forced-selection priority, same-state reset,
and the shared-state double sample during an outgoing blend. Raw licensed
assets and executable probes remain private.
